<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Later Jin (1616-1636)</h1><p class="st-meta">1616 CE – 1636 CE · capital Hetu Ala, Liaoyang, Mukden</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Jurchen khanate founded by Nurhaci, who united the Jurchen tribes and challenged the Ming, the direct forerunner of the Qing Empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a>, a chief of the Jianzhou <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, spent three decades uniting the Jurchen tribes and organising his followers into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-banners">Eight Banners</a>. In 1616 he proclaimed himself khan of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-jin-936-947">Later Jin</a> (Amaga Aisin Gurun) at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetu-ala">Hetu Ala</a>, claiming succession from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty</a> (1115-1234).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-banners">Eight Banners</a> combined military, social and administrative roles, enrolling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a>, and later Mongol and Chinese, households. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> declared war on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a> in 1618, won a great victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sarhu">Sarhu</a> in 1619, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoyang">Liaoyang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Shenyang</a> and in 1625 made Shenyang (Mukden) his capital. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> script based on Mongolian was created and later improved.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> was checked at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ningyuan">Ningyuan</a> in 1626 and died that year. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-taiji">Hong Taiji</a> brought in Chinese officials, subdued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chahar-mongols">Chahar Mongols</a>, renamed his people <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> in 1635 and in 1636 proclaimed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>, which conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from 1644.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/later-jin-1616-1636.jpg" alt="Later Jin (1616-1636)" width="320" height="205"><figcaption>Image: Evawen · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%98%8E%E6%9C%AB%E6%B8%85%E5%85%B4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1616 CE – 1636 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hetu Ala, Liaoyang, Mukden</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nurhaci, Hong Taiji</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Manchu shamanism, Tibetan Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Manchu, Mongolian, Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Later Jin (Manchu), Amaga Aisin Gurun</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1626&amp;focus=later-jin-1616-1636" aria-label="Later Jin (1616-1636) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7698 651 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Later Jin (1616-1636) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8018 907L7567 1068M8018 907L7817 927M8018 907L7768 1401"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7567 1068h0M7817 927h0M7768 1401h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8018 907h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8018 907h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1626&amp;focus=later-jin-1616-1636">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
